<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oh! Susanna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written <a href="/years/1846">1846</a>, first performed <a href="/years/1847">1847</a>, published <a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen Foster wrote &quot;Oh! Susanna&quot; in Cincinnati, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, in 1846. A local quintet first sang it at a concert in Andrews' Eagle Ice Cream Saloon in Pittsburgh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, on 11 September 1847, and W. C. Peters &amp; Co. of Cincinnati published it in 1848. Minstrel troupes made it a great success.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song blends several musical traditions. Its opening line speaks of a banjo on the singer's knee, but its beat comes from the polka, which had just reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the first two phrases of its melody are based on the major pentatonic scale. The writer and musician Glenn Weiser suggests that Foster drew on two songs published in 1846: the verse resembles Billy Whitlock's Mary Blane, and the opening of the chorus is almost identical to Silas S. Steele's Rose of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, whose story of a lover travelling from one Deep Southern state to another with his banjo in search of his sweetheart it shares.</p><p>Its original words were racist, depicting black people as dim-witted; the second verse uses a racial slur. The contradictory lines that remain in later versions, such as the sun so hot I froze to death, have been described as nonsense left after those lyrics were removed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>As was common at the time, many people registered the song for copyright under their own names, and it was copyrighted and published at least twenty-one times between 25 February 1848 and 14 February 1851. Foster earned just $100 for it. At the time no American song had sold more than 5,000 copies of sheet music, but Oh! Susanna sold over 100,000, and its success led the firm Firth, Pond &amp; Company to offer Foster two cents a copy in royalties, which persuaded him to become the first fully professional songwriter in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>With new words about travelling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> with a washpan on the singer's knee, it became an unofficial theme of the Forty-Niners of the gold rush. Harry C. Browne recorded it with the original lyrics in 1916, and later versions came from The Byrds in 1965 and James Taylor in 1970. Tim Rose's The Banjo Song of 1963 set Foster's words to a new melody, which Shocking Blue later used for Venus. In 2010 members of the Western Writers of America chose it as one of the Top 100 Western songs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oh! Susanna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
